<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the Developer ID (Homebrew) Mac Catalyst build.

  A SEPARATE FILE from PrayerApp.entitlements on purpose. That one is applied
  by Xcode at build time and uses $(AppIdentifierPrefix) / $(CFBundleIdentifier),
  which only expand during a build; this one is handed straight to `codesign`
  after the fact by scripts/build-catalyst.sh, so every value has to be
  literal. It also has to name the Catalyst bundle identifier
  (maccatalyst.com.hassan.prayerapp), which is not the identifier the iOS
  build uses.

  On the app group identifier, which is NOT the plain iOS one: Apple's own
  guidance contradicts itself here. DTS says Catalyst keeps the iOS-style
  `group.` identifier and warns that Xcode wrongly prefixes it with the Team
  ID; macOS logs that "Group containers identifiers should be prefixed by
  requestor's team ID to allow access on this platform". Settled by experiment
  on 2026-08-07, and the answer is that both are true of different processes.

  This app is not sandboxed, and with `group.com.prayerapp` it wrote the whole
  payload to ~/Library/Group Containers/group.com.prayerapp — from this side
  everything looked finished. The widget extension is the other side, and it
  must be sandboxed (macOS refuses to host an extension that isn't). Under
  that name it launched, rendered, and faulted on every read:

    Couldn't read values in CFPrefsPlistSource (Domain: group.com.prayerapp,
    Container: (null)): accessing preferences outside an application's
    container requires user-preference-read or file-read-data sandbox access

  i.e. a widget that draws an empty card. Under `GAW23HT439.group.com.prayerapp`
  that fault does not appear, so that is the name this channel uses.

  Being careful about what that does and does not establish: a probe binary
  signed with the extension's exact entitlements, plain group and all, read
  the plain container perfectly well from a shell. So this is not the blanket
  "sandboxed processes need the prefix" rule it looks like; something narrower
  about ExtensionKit's hosting is involved and is not yet understood. The
  prefixed name is the one that has never failed. MihrabAppGroup.m repeats
  this for anyone reading the code rather than the entitlements.

  Mind the shape: team ID FIRST, then `group.`. An earlier attempt spelled it
  `group.GAW23HT439.com.prayerapp`, which macOS does not recognise as prefixed
  at all — it produced no container, and that dead end is what first made the
  prefix rule look like a myth.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>GAW23HT439.group.com.prayerapp</string>
	</array>
	<!--
	  NO keychain-access-groups here, however much this build would like one.
	  It is a RESTRICTED entitlement: macOS will only honour it if the bundle
	  embeds a provisioning profile that authorises it, and a Developer ID
	  build signed outside Xcode carries no profile. Claiming it anyway does
	  not degrade — it makes the app unlaunchable. Tried on 2026-08-07 and
	  the app was SIGKILLed before main(), with amfid logging:

	    maccatalyst.com.hassan.prayerapp: Unsatisfied entitlements:
	      keychain-access-groups
	    Restricted entitlements not validated, bailing out.
	      Error … Code=-413 "No matching profile found"
	    AMFI: bailing out because of restricted entitlements.

	  `codesign` verification passes and notarization is ACCEPTED either way,
	  so neither catches this — only launching the built app does. (Careful
	  editing this comment: an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen,
	  so command flags have to be spelled out in prose. AMFI rejects the
	  whole file with "AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error" if you slip.)

	  application-groups above is NOT restricted and needs no profile, which
	  is why the widget works at all: the same launch that failed with the
	  Keychain group succeeded without it and wrote prayer_widget_payload_v1
	  into the group container.

	  The cost is that react-native-encrypted-storage cannot reach a shared
	  Keychain group, so everything that wants one falls back to plaintext
	  AsyncStorage on this channel.

	  That sentence used to say the journal, the fasting log and the
	  coordinates already did fall back, and only the last of the three
	  actually did — secureStorage.ts had its own. Everything going through
	  durableWrite.ts retried three times and threw, so a logged prayer on
	  this build had nowhere to go and sync could not start at all, having
	  no way to keep an identity. durableWrite.ts has the fallback now, and
	  a franker note about what it costs.

	  Fixing it properly is a release-process job, and the build script now
	  does its half: put a Developer ID provisioning profile for the
	  Catalyst App ID, with Keychain Sharing enabled, at
	  ios/PrayerApp/embedded.provisionprofile and build-catalyst.sh embeds
	  it, signs against Catalyst-keychain.entitlements instead of this file,
	  and asserts afterwards that the group really sealed in.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
